# A pure tone: a Wien-bridge oscillator, the circuit of Hewlett-Packard's first product, playing a sine
# through a speaker. Compare `oscillator`, whose transistor runs out of room to swing and buzzes.
#
# Q1, an NPN, and Q2, a PNP straight off its collector, each turn their input upside down, so together
# they gain a great deal the right way up; Q3 follows Q2 to drive what comes after. Two paths feed the
# output back to Q1:
#
#   * The Wien network, R1 and C1 in series then R2 and C2 to the bias, into Q1's base: at
#     1 / (2π · 10k · 22n), about 720 Hz, it passes a third of the output back exactly in step, and
#     at any other frequency less, and out of step.
#   * RF back to Q1's emitter, against RE1, holds the amplifier's gain to 1 + RF / RE1, about 3.08,
#     and its DC too: there is no capacitor in this loop but Q2's bypass, so it cannot motorboat.
#
# A loop gaining a third of 3.08 grows a little each cycle at 720 Hz and dies away at every other
# frequency, so switching it on is enough to start it. As it grows, D1 and D2 start to conduct
# through RD across RF on the peaks, lowering the gain until it is exactly 3 averaged over a cycle,
# where the swing holds, about 0.85 V, within a second or two. Nothing clips, so what settles is a
# sine, its harmonics together about 1% of it; the phase-shift oscillator's are about 9%. The closer
# the gain starts to 3 the purer the sine and the longer it takes to grow. CD keeps RF's DC out of the
# diodes.
#
# The speaker hangs off the output through CS and RV, which keep the follower's DC and most of its
# current out of it. The speaker plays while the clock runs at a second a second; `m` mutes it, and
# `r` starts it again from nothing.

speed 1

battery   B1    38 0  38 16  12V
wire            2 0   38 0
wire            2 16  38 16
ground          2 16

# The bias Q1's base sits at, held still by CB, and the Wien network's shunt arm to it.
resistor  RB1   2 0   2 6    33k
resistor  RB2   2 6   2 16   10k
wire            2 6   7 6
capacitor CB    4 6   4 16   10u
resistor  R2    6 6   6 8    10k
capacitor C2    7 6   7 8    22n
wire            6 8   8 8

# Q1, with RE1 setting its gain against RF.
resistor  RC1   10 0  10 4   4.7k
npn       Q1    10 4  8 8    10 10  2N3904
resistor  RE1   10 10 10 16  480

# Q2, a PNP straight off Q1's collector, its emitter resistor bypassed.
wire            10 4  12 4
wire            12 4  12 6
wire            12 6  14 6
resistor  RE2   16 0  16 3   1k
wire            16 3  18 3
capacitor CE2   18 0  18 3   100u
pnp       Q2    16 9  14 6   16 3   2N3906
resistor  RC2   16 9  16 16  2.4k

# Q3, the follower: the output is its emitter.
wire            16 9  19 9
wire            21 0  21 6
npn       Q3    21 6  19 9   21 12  2N3904
resistor  RE3   21 12 21 16  1k
wire            21 12 30 12

# The Wien network's series arm, from the output round underneath to Q1's base.
wire            6 8   6 18
wire            6 18  9 18
resistor  R1    9 18  12 18  10k
capacitor C1    12 18 15 18  22n
wire            15 18 28 18
wire            28 18 28 12

# RF from the output to Q1's emitter, and the diodes that level the swing across it.
wire            10 10 8 10
wire            8 10  8 20
resistor  RF    8 20  12 20  1k
wire            12 20 29 20
wire            29 20 29 12
wire            8 20  8 22
resistor  RD    8 22  10 22  4.7k
capacitor CD    10 22 13 22  10u
diode     D1    13 22 15 22
diode     D2    15 24 13 24
wire            13 22 13 24
wire            15 22 15 24
wire            15 22 15 20

# The speaker.
capacitor CS    30 12 32 12  220u
resistor  RV    32 12 35 12  1k
speaker   SPK   35 12 35 16  8

label     sine  24 12
meter     SINE  24 12 24 16
meter     SPKR  35 12 35 16
